    {"term": "Water content (WC%)", "defn": "Water content (WC%) is the part of the volume of a block or of a pot that is water at this time, as a percentage. In rockwool, the WC is the one value that you control.", "tags": ["crop steering", "precision"]},
    {"term": "Recovery floor", "defn": "The recovery floor is the lowest water content from which a rockwool block can become wet again, equally, from the dripper. At a value less than the floor (approximately 25 to 30% WC), channeling occurs in the fiber and the inner part of the block stays dry.", "tags": ["crop steering"]},
    {"term": "Dryback %", "defn": "Dryback % is the size of a dryback, in percentage points of water content. It is the peak of each day minus the next trough. For example, 78% to 58% is a dryback of 20 points.", "tags": ["crop steering"]},
    {"term": "Substrate EC", "defn": "The substrate EC is the strength of the salt in the water in the substrate, where the roots are. Use this reading for the control, and not the EC of the dripper or of the drain.", "tags": ["crop steering", "precision"]},
